sentences.l:
Long has the reproach (been), and the excess of the ignorance has tempted you; * so how often, by the writing of my hand, in the poetry, have I forbidden you!
And you increase, at the forbidding, in greed; * and I am not, except with the hiding of the secret, pleased-with you.
Hide your passion and do not proclaim it ever; * and if you speak, then indeed I am not regarding you.
And if you return to what you (are) mentioning it, * then indeed the raven of the parting has come to you, announcing-your-death;
And in a little the death will be rushing * upon you, and the burial under the earth your dwelling;
And you leave the family, O deluded one, in regret * over your parting, the length of the time, mourning you.

sentences.i:
Too long the reproaches, and your reckless folly has led you on; * how often have I warned you off in verse, in my own hand!
Yet the more I forbid you, the more you hope; * and only if you keep the secret will I be content with you.
Hide your love, and never speak it aloud; * for if you speak, I will have no regard for you.
And if you go back to what you have been saying, * the raven of parting has come already to cry your death;
Soon enough death will come rushing down * upon you, and a grave beneath the earth will be your home;
And you will leave your people, you fool, to their grief * at losing you, mourning you all their days.

sentences.l:
All of what the lovers have said of grief, * I have gathered it alone, until my endurance grew weak;
And if you want a watering-place from my tears, the seas of the tear have widened * for the comers-to-water, in abundance;
And if you want (to) see the lovers, what the hands of the passion * have done with them, then look at my body.

sentences.i:
All the grief that lovers ever told of * I have gathered in myself alone, until my strength gave way;
If you would find a watering-place in my tears, * the seas of weeping have spread wide, in full flood, for all who come to drink;
And if you would see what the hands of passion * have done to lovers, look at my body.

sentences.l:
Whoever is not loving the necks and the eyes, * then claims the pleasure of the world, he has not spoken-truly;
For in the love (is) a meaning (that) does not perceive it, * of the creation, except every one who has loved.
May God not lighten from my heart its longing * for whom I have loved, nor from my eyelid the sleeplessness.

sentences.i:
Whoever does not love slender necks and dark eyes, * and then claims to know the joys of this world, is not telling the truth;
For in love there is a meaning that no one * in all creation grasps, save those who love.
May God never ease my heart of its longing * for the one I love, nor my eyelid of its waking.

sentences.l:
I used to reckon that union with you is bought * with the noblest of the riches and the bodies;
And I thought […] that your love (is) easy, * […] upon it the precious-things of the profits;
Until I saw you pluck and single-out, from * […] […] the gifts.
[…] that you are not attained by a stratagem, * and I turned my head […] for me my wing;
And you made in me a nest (of) […] my sharer; * in it (is) my going-out-at-morning always and my coming-back-at-evening.

sentences.i:
I used to think your favour could be bought * with the choicest of riches and of living souls;
I thought […] that your love came easy, * […] the richest of profits on it;
Until I saw you gather and set apart, from * […] […] the gifts.
[…] that you are not won by any trick, * and I turned my head […] my wing;
And you made in me a nest of […], my sharer, * where I go out each morning and come home each night.
